# Prettier configuration
#
# Lifted from hc-patient-dashboard/.prettierrc @ 12e418c so that a file moved between the two repos
# reformats to the same bytes — that is what keeps `npm run provenance:check` reporting real
# divergences rather than whitespace.
#
# THAT BYTE-PARITY NO LONGER HOLDS FOR TEMPLATES, since backlog item 23 (2026-09-09). The overrides
# block at the bottom gives every `.html` here the `angular` parser and `htmlWhitespaceSensitivity:
# strict`; `web` has neither, so it formats anything that is not a `*.component.html` with the
# generic `html` parser and the default `css` sensitivity. A `*.page.html` moved between the repos
# will NOT round-trip to the same bytes, and `provenance:check` should be read with that in mind.
# Every other file type is unaffected.
#
# Divergence: the `plugins` block is dropped. The web loads prettier-plugin-java (there is no Java
# in this repo) and prettier-plugin-packagejson (not installed here). Neither affects the formatting
# of any file this project actually has.
#
# Second divergence: the `overrides` block at the bottom, which the web does not need. Read it
# before touching anything about how templates are formatted here.

printWidth: 140
singleQuote: true
tabWidth: 2
useTabs: false

# js and ts rules:
arrowParens: avoid

# jsx and tsx rules:
bracketSameLine: false

# --- Angular templates take the `angular` parser, not the generic `html` one -------------------
#
# THIS IS THE WHOLE OF WHY `*.html` WAS ONCE EXCLUDED FROM PRETTIER, AND IT IS A ONE-LINE FIX.
# Prettier picks its parser from the FILENAME: `*.component.html` infers `angular`, everything else
# infers `html`. The `angular` parser understands `@if`/`@for`/`@switch` and indents their contents;
# the generic `html` parser does not, and flattens them to the parent's level — it will even put
# `@switch (x) { @case ('a') {` on one line. This repo's templates are Ionic `*.page.html`, so they
# inferred `html` and were flattened, and backlog item 16 read that as a property of the prettier
# VERSION and pointed prettier away from every template. It is a property of parser inference. Item
# 23 (2026-09-09) corrected it and this block is the fix; `.prettierignore` no longer excludes html.
#
# Verify the inference rather than trusting this comment, and pass `--ignore-path /dev/null` or
# prettier reports `"ignored": true` and tells you nothing:
#
#     npx prettier --ignore-path /dev/null --file-info src/app/portal/profile/profile.page.html
#     → { "ignored": false, "inferredParser": "angular" }        (with this block)
#     → { "ignored": false, "inferredParser": "html" }           (without it)
#
# `excludeFiles` rather than `files: '*.page.html'`, which is what item 23 proposed: every .html in
# this repo IS an Angular template except the document shell, and naming the one exception closes the
# hole that listing the conventions would leave — a template called anything else would silently take
# the flattening parser again, which is the exact defect this block exists to end.
#
# THE TRADE THAT CHOICE MAKES, because it is not free. `files: '*.page.html'` could only ever miss a
# template; this can only ever CATCH A NON-TEMPLATE. Add a plain HTML file under src/ — an email
# body, a static legal page — and it gets the angular parser, which parses `{{ … }}` as an Angular
# interpolation and will rewrite the inside of it, with `prettier:check` calling the result correct.
# The remedy is one line: add it to `excludeFiles` alongside the shell. There is no such file today;
# `src/index.html` is the only non-template `.html` in the repo.
#
# `src/index.html` is EXCLUDED FROM THIS OVERRIDE, NOT FROM PRETTIER. It is still checked and still
# formatted — with the generic `html` parser and the default whitespace sensitivity, which is right
# for a document that is not an Angular template. A stray space in it fails `prettier:check` today,
# where under item 16's `*.html` ignore rule it did not.
#
# --- htmlWhitespaceSensitivity: strict, and what it does and does not buy ----------------------
#
# THIS APP SETS `angularCompilerOptions.preserveWhitespaces: true` (tsconfig.json:46). Whitespace
# text nodes are NOT stripped at compile time here — they reach the DOM, and only CSS decides
# whether they render. So "the reformat was whitespace-only" is not on its own an argument that
# nothing changed, and the default `css` sensitivity is the setting that makes it one only by
# coincidence: prettier decides where it may add or remove whitespace from a BUILT-IN TABLE OF TAG
# display defaults, while this stylesheet carries 52 `display:` declarations, seven of which make an
# element inline-level (.hc-btn, .hc-pill, .hc-chip, .hc-fchip, .hc-select, .hc-legend,
# .hc-viz__tip-key). Prettier cannot see any of them, and the gate would not report a collision.
#
# NOTHING COLLIDES TODAY, and that was checked site by site rather than in aggregate. Of the 15
# templates whose AST moves, almost all of it is indentation DEPTH inside a text node, which cannot
# render — any whitespace run containing a newline collapses to one space. Reduced to changes of
# PRESENCE, there are NINE sites in nine templates: two text/interpolation edges and seven
# containers gaining a whitespace node. Every one of the nine is the same shape — a container with a
# single child going from `[X]` to `[BLANK, X, BLANK]`, whitespace at the EDGE of the box, never
# between two element siblings, which is the only arrangement that renders. The containers are
# `<p>` and `<div>` carrying .hc-auth-foot, .hc-sm, .hc-prose and .hc-mt-16; none of those four sets
# `display` at all, so all nine are block boxes and CSS strips edge whitespace in every one.
#
# `strict` tells prettier every element is whitespace-sensitive, so it wraps with the
# `<div class="x"\n  >text</div\n>` form rather than by inserting a newline inside the tag. MEASURED
# ON THE COMMITTED TREE: adopting it changes ZERO FILES. It costs nothing now and constrains every
# future reflow, which is the whole reason it is here.
#
# BE PRECISE ABOUT THE LIMIT, because the obvious claim for it is wrong. `strict` does NOT make the
# reformat semantically null. Re-indenting the body of an `@if` moves whitespace text nodes whatever
# this option says, so parsing the pre-item-23 templates with `preserveWhitespaces: true` and
# comparing: `css` changes 15 of 29 templates, `strict` changes 10. It cuts the exposure by a third
# and does not remove it, and no prettier setting can while templates are being re-indented at all.
#
# WHY THE COMMITTED TREE WAS NOT REFORMATTED AGAIN UNDER IT. Re-running from the pre-item-23
# templates with `strict` would leave those five extra templates' DOM untouched — profile.page.html
# among them, the one item 16 existed to protect — at the price of 9 more files rewritten and the
# bracket form spreading from 76 lines to 97. All nine renderable sites have been checked and none
# can render, so that trade spends real readability to buy down a risk already measured as null.
# Recorded here so the option stays visible rather than forgotten: if a `display:` override ever
# does collide with prettier's table, re-running the reformat under `strict` is the lever.
overrides:
  - files: '*.html'
    excludeFiles: 'src/index.html'
    options:
      parser: angular
      # See the block above — NOT cosmetic. This is what stops a future reflow from silently
      # moving a whitespace text node into or out of the DOM under `preserveWhitespaces: true`.
      htmlWhitespaceSensitivity: strict
